# Shell scripts MUST be LF, always, regardless of who checks them out.
#
# Git for Windows converts LF to CRLF on checkout by default. A shell script with
# CRLF has a shebang of `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and Linux looks for an
# interpreter literally named "bash\r", which does not exist. The error is
# `env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory` and it took a failed Cloud Build to
# find, because the script runs perfectly on the Windows machine that wrote it.
#
# This cost a real outage: every Cloud Build smoke test failed at step 4, so no
# revision could be promoted and production silently stayed on an old one while
# builds appeared to run.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else a Linux container reads and executes or parses
# by line.
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf

# Windows tooling owns these, so leave them CRLF.
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.bat  text eol=crlf

# Never touch binaries.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.ico  binary
